 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "TAOB.2 (learning outcome) Making meaning from visual materials. TAOB.2.a: Connects the information in visual reading materials with daily life (works out what to do in the kitchen from the soap, fruit, bowl and heart pictures on the recipe card). Makes predictions from pictures: “What does the soap picture say? Wash your hands, or wash the dishes?”",
   "HSAB.7 (learning outcome) Showing healthy eating behaviours in daily life (washes hands and fruit before preparing food; helps to prepare a healthy snack from colourful fruit; tells fruit from vegetables).",
   "OB4 Visual Literacy: notices that pictures and symbols carry a message and that a picture card is read in order, from left to right and top to bottom; produces a visual message by drawing their own picture recipe.",
   "SDB2.2 Cooperation: completes a shared task by sharing jobs; Dad chops, Deniz reads, counts and stirs.",
   "D16 Responsibility: takes on the job of “recipe reader” and keeps it up until the task is done; reminds Dad of the card when he rushes."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show the board on the cover: “There are no words on this card. What do you think it tells us?”",
   "Introduce the word “recipe”: “A recipe tells you step by step how to make a dish. Have you ever cooked from a recipe at home?”",
   "Recall picture signs in the classroom or at home (the hand-washing picture, the bin sign, traffic lights): “Can these be read too?”"
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, whistle “fwee-fwoo” together with Dad.",
   "On page 2, talk about why Deniz's shoulders drooped; then touch the card pictures in order and “read” them with Deniz. Trace the path from left to right and top to bottom with your finger.",
   "On page 3, talk about both options in the prediction: “What might the soap picture mean? What is soap for when we cook?”",
   "On page 5, count the fruit together, pointing with a finger; stop at the carrot: “Is a carrot a fruit or a vegetable?”"
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Talk about visual reading with the question “How did Deniz read the recipe? How did she understand it without knowing the letters?”",
   "“Why does the heart picture mean ‘let's eat together’? Which picture would you draw for this step?” Talk about how we agree on the meaning of picture signs together.",
   "Put the recipe steps in order: first wash hands, then count and chop the fruit, and finally eat together. What happens if the order changes?",
   "Recall how the jobs were shared: what did Dad do, what did Deniz do? How did the job go when they worked together?",
   "Give the child a chance to share their view by asking “What did you think? If you were Deniz, which job would you want?”"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Recipe Readers",
   "sure": "25 min",
   "materyal": "Blank cards with four sections, pastel crayons, washed fruit (banana, apple, tangerine, grapes), a bowl, children's spoons; a ready-made 4-step picture card for a fruit plate",
   "adim": [
    "Game: the teacher shows a picture card (soap, fruit, bowl, heart); the children act out what the card says: soap → rub hands, bowl → stir, heart → hug.",
    "Pin the picture recipe card on the board. The children take turns to “read” one picture and say what it means (TAOB.2).",
    "Hand out jobs such as recipe reader, counter and stirrer; after washing hands, prepare the fruit plate together following the card. The teacher cuts hard fruit; children can break soft fruit like banana with a plastic child's knife or their hands.",
    "Each child draws their own 4-picture recipe (yoghurt drink, fruit plate, cheese on bread…). A friend “reads” the recipe without words (OB4).",
    "Note: get allergy information from families before any food activity."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Why did Deniz say “I can't read”? What did she discover next?",
   "What was the first picture in the recipe? Why do we wash our hands before making food?",
   "Which job would you like in the kitchen: reading, counting or stirring?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Fridge Recipe",
   "d": "Draw a 3–4 step picture recipe with your child (a fruit plate, a yoghurt drink, fruit with yoghurt) and stick it on the fridge. While you prepare it, let your child “read” the recipe and follow the order they say. You take on the knife and the cooker; washing, counting and stirring are their jobs."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Deniz's Picture Recipe”. Deniz is sad because she thinks she can't read the recipe without knowing the letters; then she discovers that pictures can be read too and becomes the kitchen's “recipe reader”. In the preschool years, making sense of pictures, signs and symbols is the first step towards reading. At home, draw a simple picture recipe and let your child read it; giving them a small but real job strengthens their sense of responsibility."
 },
